        {
          "id": "SP_LEUCOSOLENIA_SALPINX_VAN_SOEST_2017",
          "name": "Leucosolenia salpinx Van Soest, 2017",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Leucosolenia",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "(Figs 126 a – d) Groups of tiny, individual, olynthus-type tubes attached to the tails of Tetilla pentatriaena specimens. Shape trumpet-like (Figs 126 a – b), tapering from a wide apical aperture to a thin attachment tail. Apical aperture surrounded by a fringe of trichoxeas (Fig. 126 d). Tube body hairy (Fig. 126 a), with long single trichoxeas and microdiactines protruding at right or oblique angles from the body. Length of tubes 2 – 3 mm, width of the tubes 200 – 250 µm. Color in alcohol transparent-whitish. Skeleton. (Figs 126 c – d) Undifferentiated skeleton consisting of tangential sagittal triactines, tetractines and long thin trichoxeas, together forming the wall of the ascon-type sponge body. Long trichoxeas curve out from the skeleton and protrude far beyond the surface. Microdiactines are lodged in the skeleton between the triactines. Towards the thin tapering end, long thin trichoxeas become proportionally dominant, and at the end only trichoxeas are found in the skeleton. Spicules. (Figs 127 a – e) Triactines, tetractines, trichoxeas, microdiactines. Triactines, sagittal, with straight, long, unpaired actine, divisible more or less clearly into (1) those with short, recurved, paired actines and the longest unpaired actines (Fig.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:9066906",
